berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1.2 and berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "heat exists in Matter, without the mind, as a pattern of a real being" and "heat appears various to different perceivers or at different stations while the object remains unaltered" are true and "sweetness exists in Matter, without the mind, as a pattern of a real being", "sweetness appears various to different perceivers or at different stations while the object remains unaltered", "figure exists in Matter, without the mind, as a pattern of a real being", "figure appears various to different perceivers or at different stations while the object remains unaltered", "extension exists in Matter, without the mind, as a pattern of a real being", "extension appears various to different perceivers or at different stations while the object remains unaltered", "motion exists in Matter, without the mind, as a pattern of a real being" and "motion appears various to different perceivers or at different stations while the object remains unaltered" are false. Is S2, "Thus, for instance, it is said that heat and cold are affections only of the mind, and not at all patterns of real beings, existing in the corporeal substances which excite them, for that the same body which appears cold to one hand seems warm to another.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
